555
00:25:30.240 --> 00:25:33.660
We had a fan question from Evan Warburton.

556
00:25:34.400 --> 00:25:41.380
He says, Angela, did it make it easier or more difficult to film a scene when the characters

557
00:25:41.380 --> 00:25:42.940
are back-to-back?

558
00:25:43.960 --> 00:25:47.520
Because I know that Rain and Angela did quite a few scenes like this.

559
00:25:47.740 --> 00:25:52.020
Like, did it make it easier to stay in character because you didn't have to make eye contact?

560
00:25:52.360 --> 00:25:53.280
What was it like?

561
00:25:53.280 --> 00:25:54.180
Tell us, Angela.

562
00:25:54.720 --> 00:25:55.600
Well, I'll tell you this.

563
00:25:55.740 --> 00:26:00.800
The biggest thing about these scenes that I remember was timing because you cannot see

564
00:26:00.800 --> 00:26:02.020
the other person speaking.

565
00:26:03.240 --> 00:26:07.860
So, you know, you don't respond quite the same way because you want to make sure they finish

566
00:26:07.860 --> 00:26:10.640
their sentence or sort of whatever they're doing.

567
00:26:10.640 --> 00:26:11.920
They're emoting, right?

568
00:26:12.100 --> 00:26:12.940
Whatever their thing is.

569
00:26:12.940 --> 00:26:19.100
So I remember the first time we did it, there were probably more pauses because we wanted

570
00:26:19.100 --> 00:26:22.440
to sort of make sure we had the give and take because naturally when you talk to someone,

571
00:26:22.620 --> 00:26:23.840
you're reading their face.

572
00:26:24.420 --> 00:26:28.600
And so it was sort of just like acting off of someone's voice as well.

573
00:26:28.720 --> 00:26:30.920
Like all of my reacting was in listening.

574
00:26:31.280 --> 00:26:37.840
I think in some ways it made us more thoughtful performers because we couldn't face the other

575
00:26:37.840 --> 00:26:42.640
person and we had to really listen to them and react off the last thing they said.

576
00:26:42.640 --> 00:26:45.160
Did it make it hard to improvise?

577
00:26:46.380 --> 00:26:47.340
Oh, for sure.

578
00:26:47.500 --> 00:26:53.380
I remember really being conscious of not improvising any lines within a written scene and anything

579
00:26:53.380 --> 00:26:58.780
that I did improvise came at the very end when I knew I wouldn't be stepping on anything

580
00:26:58.780 --> 00:26:59.340
Rain said.

581
00:26:59.560 --> 00:27:03.780
So most of my improv in these scenes, if it made it in, was something I said as I was leaving

582
00:27:03.780 --> 00:27:06.080
or at the very beginning.

583
00:27:06.300 --> 00:27:07.100
That makes sense.

584
00:27:07.420 --> 00:27:09.460
So yeah, so they have this scene in the kitchen.

