690
00:31:45.580 --> 00:31:51.120
We actually got so many questions about Call of Duty, and this seemed like the perfect one to get us started.

691
00:31:51.400 --> 00:31:55.360
As you guys know, Jenna and I were not in those scenes at Stanford.

692
00:31:55.360 --> 00:31:56.600
That's right.

693
00:31:57.000 --> 00:32:02.980
So, Shelby, I reached out to a lot of people to get you the real deal on all things Call of Duty.

694
00:32:03.300 --> 00:32:06.940
Angela, I got all the scoop from our line producer, Kent Sabornak.

695
00:32:07.280 --> 00:32:12.860
I texted with our editor, Dean Holland, and Andy Bernard himself, Ed HelMs.

696
00:32:13.080 --> 00:32:15.300
Lainey, that is a serious deep dive.

697
00:32:15.440 --> 00:32:15.940
I like it.

698
00:32:16.000 --> 00:32:16.580
I like it.

699
00:32:16.700 --> 00:32:17.660
I deep dived.

700
00:32:17.780 --> 00:32:18.540
I really did.

701
00:32:18.720 --> 00:32:19.780
Okay, so what did you find out?

702
00:32:19.780 --> 00:32:29.060
All right, so Kent told me that after we wrapped season one of the show, the post-production guys moved to some offices in Venice to edit the episodes.

703
00:32:29.360 --> 00:32:41.120
And while they were there, Kent, just for whatever reason, just on a whim, installed Call of Duty on everyone's computers, just so they could blow off steam during edit sessions.

704
00:32:41.120 --> 00:32:50.880
And then when we moved to our new stages for season two, he did it again, but this time he also installed it on all of the writers' computers.

705
00:32:50.920 --> 00:32:56.020
And that is when the editors and the writers started playing one another.

706
00:32:56.220 --> 00:32:59.840
I remember this because our editors and writers were in the same building.

707
00:33:00.120 --> 00:33:01.840
They were over where the warehouse is, right?

708
00:33:02.080 --> 00:33:04.560
And the writers were upstairs and the editors were downstairs.

709
00:33:04.560 --> 00:33:09.880
And sometimes I would go over there at lunch because I was friends with Claire Scanlon, one of our editors at the time.

710
00:33:10.040 --> 00:33:14.300
And I would walk in the editing bay and they were all like, get down!

711
00:33:14.580 --> 00:33:15.340
Over here!

712
00:33:15.640 --> 00:33:16.960
I was like, what is happening?

713
00:33:18.460 --> 00:33:21.860
I remember going up to the writers' room and seeing the same thing.

714
00:33:22.040 --> 00:33:27.920
They had this big conference table in the writers' room and they would have all their computers in a circle.

715
00:33:27.920 --> 00:33:34.240
And then some people had little offices and then some people would be around this conference room table.

716
00:33:34.440 --> 00:33:35.480
And it was the same thing.

717
00:33:35.640 --> 00:33:37.480
They did exactly like what Stanford did.

718
00:33:37.560 --> 00:33:38.820
They would turn off the lights.

719
00:33:38.820 --> 00:33:39.080
Yeah.

720
00:33:39.360 --> 00:33:42.740
And they would have this elaborate match going on.

721
00:33:42.880 --> 00:33:44.460
Oh my gosh, they were so into it.

722
00:33:44.780 --> 00:33:47.980
Well, I guess Greg Daniels was particularly obsessed.

723
00:33:47.980 --> 00:33:57.920
In fact, our editor, Dean Holland, told me that the very first time he met with Greg to start editing an episode, within about 10 minutes, Greg said,

724
00:33:58.600 --> 00:33:59.840
have you ever played Call of Duty?

725
00:34:00.280 --> 00:34:01.700
And Dean was like, yeah.

726
00:34:02.220 --> 00:34:05.320
And he said, Greg left the room and started a 20-person game.

727
00:34:06.280 --> 00:34:07.820
Like within 10 minutes.

728
00:34:08.240 --> 00:34:12.560
How perfect that Greg was the person that directed this episode because he loves Call of Duty.

729
00:34:12.820 --> 00:34:13.460
I know.

730
00:34:13.580 --> 00:34:14.100
He loves it.

731
00:34:14.200 --> 00:34:16.020
I don't think that was a mistake.

732
00:34:16.880 --> 00:34:17.360
No.

733
00:34:17.980 --> 00:34:18.360
No.

734
00:34:18.780 --> 00:34:19.260
All right.

